speechtext:
Yes, they are;
they are absolutely conjoined. It is impossible for the United States properly to arrange for the certification of invoices of exports from Canada unless she gives fair facilities to all parts of the country. For instance, it would not be fair for the United States or Canada to introduce and put into active operation a scheme of that kind, involving the payment of fees, unless it made it applicable to the whole country. To do that it employs two agencies; it has its own consuls general and consuls, and it has its own agents and representatives, and it is quite possible that in Canada about 50 per cent of the agents of the United States are such as my hon. friend represents. They are Canadians who live in the localities and they do this work for the United States besides representing it in other ways. But the other half of the agents of the United States are absolutely trade officers. Their function is not only to put their names to certificates in connection with invoices, but they are supposed to be on the lookout from day to day to find out what are the currents, the possibilities, and the openings of trade. It must be remembered that in every case the certificated invoice finds it way to headquarters at Washington, the information is gathered of the sources, the trend, and the quality of trade, being supplied to the commercial headquarters in the United States from every quarter of Canada. My hon. friend can see in a moment that that is very important information to have hold of. It is the kind of information which the
United States gets from both classes of its representatives, and the efficacy and possible result make that very eligible information and much to be desired.

That is a fixed amount;
there is no variation at present under the regulation. Now, the changes in the existing law are these:	We propose to apply
the principle of a money qualification, not only to immigrants but to non-immigrants as well, that is, to tourists, travellers, artists, etc., because we found case after case, within the course of the last year, where people of these classes had got into Canada and become a public charge. We are simply taking power, if advisable, to fix a money qualification for such people. I do not know that the power will have to be used to any extent, but it might be advisable to apply that regulation to certain of these non-immigrant classes. There is one other change in the section. The law now provides that the amount may vary according to race, occupation, or destination. We propose to add the word "nationality."
